{quote}_Regarding your suggestion to include the indemnity clause, could you please elaborate on when it could be enforced? I struggle to come up with a situation in which it could be used. Just to give you some background, generally the message from business is that the landlord (being a Polish state-owned company) hardly ever allows for any negotiations of the contracts so we should include in our markup only the crucial issues. Also, there will be a tender process for this agreement, so a very hard markup won’t help us._{quote}

Let’s say we share a Bolt employee (“*Person X*”) signs the agreement, and so is Person X’s contact details is shared with PKP. A week later, PKP suffers a data breach, resulting in Person X’s details falling into the hands of a scammer. The scammer then uses these details to harass / financially harm Person X. It is possible that Person X would go after Bolt (being the organisation most readily available to Person X) for failing to keep their data safe. In such circumstances, we would want to make sure that Bolt can recover losses from PKP for the data breach. Does that make sense?

In any case, if we suspect that no negotiations would be allowed, then provided the business understands the risks, we can proceed without it. Particularly given that the risk profile here is not high 🙂

