{quote}if the courier is blocked by ADM for initial 72 hours (investigation), and then as a result of the investigation, the courier is permanently terminated (by a human), then this human decision is not AMD, no?{quote}

Correct. If after the initial 72-hour block, human reviews the case and decides to block, *this permanent block is not ADM as a human makes a final decision*. 

However, if *the system extends the initial 72-hour block* as there is no human input yet, it (the whole initial block for 72 hours and its extension) *would likely be an ADM* as only the system is involved in the blocking and extension.

If there is *a human who extends the 72-hour block*, it *would unlikely be an ADM,* if the human is involved in the extension and does it *based on a justified reason* (the agent understands that the courier will most likely be permanently blocked). Otherwise, if a human just extends because we still were not able to investigate a case, eg no one knows whether the courier is at fault or not or whether it was a false-positive, then it may be an ADM as there is no human decision, just human action to click ‘extend’ 🙂 

{color:#0747a6}*To not complicate things with ADM, the only logic agents need to follow is to ensure they are able to review the block before it reaches 72 hours, and resort to the extension only when there is a good justification, like discovered repeated frauds and the team needs confirmation from the fraud team or others before making a final decision.* {color}

{quote}I think there will be some cases where the 72 hours is the whole duration of the block, i.e. the block will be e.g. 24 hours and will end before it reaches a human for review after 72 hours. Is that ok?{quote}

Yes, that’s totally fine.

