Hi [~accountid:712020:1a3a17cb-2e37-4162-8d95-4d1059255d7e] - it's true that B2B and B2C direct marketing can have different rules.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
In PT, the following applies to B2B (restaurant) marketing:

# Opt-in when sent to an individualised email.
# Opt-out when non-individualised email.
# Additionally, B2B recipients included in the Robinson list below should be excluded from marketing comms: [+https://www.consumidor.gov.pt/informacao-publicidade/publicidade-nao-solicitada-pessoas-coletivas.aspx+|https://www.consumidor.gov.pt/informacao-publicidade/publicidade-nao-solicitada-pessoas-coletivas.aspx]+.+
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
However, in this case, as I understand, its not about sending direct marketing to restaurants, its about automatically including these restaurants to direct marketing comms *to eaters*, and giving the restaurants the option to opt-out, correct?

In this case, I believe we can argue that the B2B direct marketing rules do not apply, and that the automated inclusions of restaurants in comms to eaters and sending comms with option to opt out is a different, business, activity - not direct marketing. I will separately catch up with my colleague to confirm this, but this,  in my view, is the most viable strategy.
{panel}

