Hi [~accountid:634fb944c97f5473af6e396c] please see my responses below:

* In a discussion with the Workers Labor Inspectorate, who is allowed to sign for fines and findings on behalf of the company? 

{color:#36b37e}Legal: The protocol is signed by the labor inspector conducting the inspection and the person or body representing the inspected entity, i.e. on behalf of Bolt Services the protocol can be signed either by the Management Board (Ahto Kink) or by the person granted with a respective power-of-attoreny authorising him/her to represent Bolt in this type of proceedings. {color}

{color:#36b37e}As regads the fines, they can be imposed either on the employer (Bolt) or on an employee (person acting on behalf of the employer, person in charge of workplace safety, or someone managing employees or others) – depending on the case / specifics of the offense. If the fine is imposed on the employer, the a person authorised to represent Bolt shall sign, but if the fine is imposed on an employee, then this particular employee shall sign / reject the fine. {color}

* Is it mandatory for that person to have a PoA or can anyone sign on behalf of the company accepting the fine and findings?

{color:#36b37e}Legal: Please see our answer above - generally the protocol must be signed by a person authorised to act on behalf of Bolt (acting in capacity of a Management Board member or commercial proxy (if appointed) or based on the relevant PoA). As regards the fines, it depends on whether the fine is imposed on the employer (Bolt) or an employee. {color}

* If the person with the PoA signs, it is normal for them to receive the fine on their personal name and pay it themselves (even if the findings are company related)?

{color:#36b37e}Legal: As above - it depends who the offender is - the employer (company) or the employee. {color}

* In the protocol that I've attached, it says that the signature of the company representative can be electronical signature as well, does that mean that if the managing director is not present in the physical meeting, it can be signed at a later time and sent to the inspectorate?

{color:#36b37e}Legal: The employer has the right to read the protocol and submit written comments within 7 days of its preparation. The protocol does not have to be signed on the spot. {color}

{color:#36b37e}The signing of the protocol usually takes place at the company headquarters, at the last meeting of the parties. We do not have to sign the inspection protocol during this meeting. It is enough for us to acknowledge receipt of the protocol for the time being and to familiarize ourselves with its content, by signing the protocol in the part of the protocol starting with the words “On ... I received a copy of it” - as below: {color}

!image-20250326-113919.png|width=564,height=147,alt="image-20250326-113919.png"!

{color:#36b37e}Only by signing in the second place (at the very end of the protocol, after the instructions ({color}{color:#36b37e}_pl: pouczenie_{color}{color:#36b37e})) does it mean that you accept its contents. We can therefore simply acknowledge receipt of the protocol and inform the inspector that we will sign it within 7 days. This is the amount of time we have to make reasoned objections to the inspection protocol. The 7-day deadline for raising objections is non-negotiable. After its expiry, the inspector considers that we fully agree with his findings.{color}

